# Valve's arm64 Steam client and Steam Runtime: the CHANNELS they are downloaded from (installed from
# Main menu > Emulators > Steam Client by steam-install; the runtime then kept on its channel by every
# start of /usr/bin/steam).
#
# Read by /usr/lib/retroforge/steam-bootstrap, which /usr/bin/steam runs on every start. Neither the
# client build nor the runtime snapshot is pinned to a version here: both channels are rolling
# pointers Valve moves, and the bootstrap installs whatever Valve publishes on them when it downloads.
# Every download is still checked: the client against the sha2 its channel's manifest gives, the
# runtime against the SHA256SUMS of the snapshot the channel names. Nothing of either is in the image
# (a root slot is 2 GB; they unpack to ~560 MB), so they go into the player's home.
#
# WHY CHANNELS AND NOT VERSIONS. Both version pins went stale on their own. The client's package files
# are content-addressed and replaced on every client release, so a pinned one stops existing at the
# next one. Valve prunes old runtime snapshots too (the oldest left on 2026-10-15 was
# 3c.0.20260526), so a pinned snapshot sets up fine until the day it is gone and then fails every
# fresh card. And the client updates itself from its channel anyway, so a pinned build would only
# have been the starting point of that update.
#
# client_channel:  the arm64 client line the bootstrap installs from: the manifest
#                  steam_client_<client_channel>_linuxarm64, and its bins_linuxarm64_linuxarm64
#                  package (steamrtarm64/). It is also written to <Steam root>/package/beta, the
#                  file the client reads its own line from.
#
#                  THIS IS COUPLED TO THE LAUNCH FLAG, and the client enforces it, not us. The OGUI
#                  Steam plugin starts `steam` with -steamdeck (rootfs/overlay/usr/bin/steam). The
#                  client OWNS package/beta and repoints it to a line that matches the flag it was
#                  started with. -steamdeck keeps either steamdeck_ line; a line it does not accept
#                  gets repointed, and that costs a ~631 MB client download on the device (measured
#                  upstream on 2026-08-19, by starting a seed of another line with -steamdeck). So
#                  the bootstrap writes package/beta when it installs the client, and when the file
#                  is missing, and otherwise never touches it: once the client runs, the line is
#                  the client's. Change this and the flag together or not at all.
#
#                  Measured 2026-10-16: steamdeck_stable and Valve's default arm64 line
#                  (steam_client_linuxarm64, which the bootstrap read before) both serve build
#                  1791918634; steamdeck_publicbeta serves 1792192922.
#
#                  WHY steamdeck_stable and not steamdeck_publicbeta: publicbeta used to be the only
#                  line that gave an arm64 client what a Windows title needs. Stable at 1785799196
#                  lacked all three (Valve's FEX compat tool 3127680, the arm64 Proton 11 offer, and
#                  Steam Linux Runtime app 4185400); from 1788291500 it has them (measured upstream on
#                  hardware, 2026-09-02). If a Windows title stops launching after a client update,
#                  look for steamapps/appmanifest_4185400.acf first; setting this to
#                  steamdeck_publicbeta is the way back. Those runtimes install lazily (4185400 only
#                  once a Proton title is launched), so an empty steamapps/ on a fresh card means
#                  nothing.
#
#                  NEVER the -deckard line, although it serves newer builds. Its client asserts
#                  Steam Frame identity, which strips the guided tour's button handlers and leaves a
#                  fresh card on a welcome screen that cannot be dismissed. Its name is a gated beta
#                  branch key rather than a line name, so Valve can rotate it at any time. And under
#                  -steamdeck the client repoints away from it anyway: that is the 631 MB case above.
#
# runtime_base:    the steamrt3c image root. <base>/<snapshot>/steam-runtime-steamrt-arm64.tar.xz
#                  is the arm64 runtime the client runs in, and <base>/<snapshot>/SHA256SUMS its
#                  sha256. The client needs it: it loads steamui.so, which needs GTK2 and the rest
#                  of the runtime, and it strips LD_LIBRARY_PATH, so it has to run inside the
#                  runtime's container (docs/steam-arm64.md). This is not the runtime in the client's
#                  own manifest: that one's runtime_steamrt_linuxarm64 is the x86_64/i386 runtime for
#                  FEX games.
#
# runtime_channel: a rolling pointer under runtime_base: <base>/<channel>.txt holds one snapshot id
#                  (3c.0.20260824.257593 on 2026-10-16, the same as
#                  latest-steam-client-general-availability). The bootstrap reads it on every start
#                  (one small GET, short timeout) and resolves it to that CONCRETE snapshot, never
#                  downloading through the alias directory: the id names the folder it installs
#                  into, so it can tell a moved channel from an unchanged one, and the log says which
#                  build is in use. A moved channel means a ~52 MB download before Steam starts.
#                  Offline, the newest installed snapshot is used.
#
# runtime_snapshot: (optional, not set) pins one snapshot id instead of following runtime_channel,
#                  for a reproducible setup. RETROFORGE_STEAM_RUNTIME_SNAPSHOT=<id> in the
#                  environment does the same and wins over this field.
client_channel: steamdeck_stable
runtime_base: https://repo.steampowered.com/steamrt3c/images
runtime_channel: latest-public-stable
